| `scripts/config_loader.py` | The one way a configured value enters a process: `KEYS` declares each key of config/project.json and config/practices.json a reader resolves, with its type and its default or none; `read` is the one open and parse of a config file, and `resolve` gives each row its value, writing a `[using default]` line for a default it takes and one line per wrong or unset key before it raises, so a config fault names every key at once. Decoder recursion raises ConfigUnreadable naming the file, and recursion copying a configured value raises WrongValue naming source and key. Diagnostic rendering hides excessive nesting after checking for an @, and deduplicates unrenderable members by identity. | imported; `tests/integration/test_config_reads_gate.py` holds every other reader to it | [`config_reads.roots`](settings.md#configprojectjson) | Never ends the process: the entry point that catches its raise exits `CONFIG` (14). Limits follow the interpreter's recursion budget; a default's copy remains unguarded because it is code. Not a schema validator of a whole file: it judges the rows a reader asks for. Stays stdlib-only and 3.6-safe, because `scripts/child_env.py` reads through it under the hook interpreter. | [limits-and-troubleshooting.md](../guides/limits-and-troubleshooting.md) | always |
